  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Which of the following shows the graph of $y=-{x}^{2}+10x-28?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's construct a table of values for the function $y=-{x}^{2}+10x-28.$ We fill the first row with the $x$-values $x=3,4,5,6,7,8,$ and then use the formula $y=-{x}^{2}+10x-28$ to compute the corresponding $y$-values in the second row.\n\n| $\\,\\,x\\,\\,$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\,y\\,\\,$ | $-7$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-7$ | $-12$ |\n\nIf we plot those points on a graph, we can start to see the shape of a parabola:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/b762d214dbd72555b069846ca8e7bcb5248c73c15db10f06018167ff7966d6d0.png)\n\nWe fill out the graph by drawing the parabola through the plotted points:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/24aae5c27fc48b7a340e36f6a54d9d6d636644c938e5328c1a8c68713179a947.png)",
